  {
    "episode_id": 81053,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81053.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Well, I did indeed be my wife there. She said, so it worked out great for me. I'm not sure that her family still thinks it was a great year for her. But it was a great year for me. And we're coming up on the 10 years of marriage this month. Now, you know, clearing the court, and then the thing about getting to work at the Supreme Court is that you get to see so many cases, first of all, because the court gets about 10,000 requests a year to hear cases, and then that's how many requests come in from the lower courts, and they only hear, well, heck, this year they only heard 50 some, my year they heard about 70 cases, so a small, small fraction of the cases they get requests for, do they actually hear Grant's searches? is a formal term for that. So you just see so many cases, and you get to learn so much about different parts of the law, and you get to see great lawyers, too. I mean, you know, the arguments, I went to every argument that we had at the court that year, and you know, it's just fascinating. You learn so much, and I've got tremendous respect. for the court of the institution. I'm not very happy with this year. That's a different subject. But, you know, I learned a lot and it made a big difference to me. That's the kind of law I went on to practice and I became a constitutional lawyer. My wife as well, I felt like my wife still practiced. And that's really my experience of the court really shaped that.",
