    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I needed to go and see it for myself. That's the reason I went. I had talked to the protesters from afar, you know, from here in the States. But I just decided that, you know what, I've got to see this. So I went to Hong Kong today, and I did. I didn't want to stay at Coop's up in an hotel somewhere. We got out on the streets much to the consternation of, I think, some of the diplomatic corps, but there you go. So we went out and we're out on the streets to the protesters in several different locations. And, you know, saw what was going on with the real deal. And I will just say that, you know, seeing up close and personal what the authoritarian regime in Beijing was willing to do and were doing to their own people, not allowing them to assemble. You know, we take for granted a lot of our rights in the United States. In the United States, we allow folks to assemble and protest peacefully, even a lot of times without a permit. I mean, you know, technically, in most places, you're supposed to get a permit. But, you know, here in the U.S., a lot of times, like, hey, you know, if it's peaceful, we let you do it. They're in Hong Kong, and this is back in October. You couldn't even get a permit. I mean, these protesters were asking, would you please give us a permit which they were entitled to under a lot? Nope. Then they were clubbing them down. They were spraying them down. They were arresting teenagers. throwing them into prison, beating them viciously. And I saw it. I saw all of this on the streets. And so it just, to me, it was such a vivid",
